{
front "Kotlin"
prose <<'MD'
# Kotlin

The matrix is declared in annotations and KSP generates the dispatcher, the
`Cells` interface with one member per `HANDLE` cell and effect, and the
effect-handler surface. The generated code is never committed; the runtime,
`tabular-center-core`, compiles against an empty classpath -- no dependencies, not
even coroutines.

## The matrix

`@Machine` names the types; each `@Row` is a state; each `CellSpec` a cell. The
annotated interface's single `handle` is the **prototype**: every generated
member copies its modifiers, receiver and visibility.

MD
code $K/examples/06-generated/src/Stopwatch.tb.kt kotlin '^@Machine\(' '^\}'
prose <<'MD'
The types it names are ordinary sealed hierarchies:

MD
code $K/examples/06-generated/src/StopwatchTypes.kt kotlin '^internal sealed interface S' '^\}'
prose <<'MD'
## The code you write

KSP turns the two `HANDLE` cells and the one effect into three abstract members.
Because the prototype is `fun Clock.handle(...)`, every one of them is an
extension on `Clock` -- a cell calls `now()` without being handed a clock, and
nothing can dispatch without one in scope:

MD
code $K/examples/06-generated/src/StopwatchImpl.kt kotlin '^internal class StopwatchImpl'
prose <<'MD'
Receivers, `suspend`, `internal`, `@Composable` -- whatever the prototype
carries, the generated surface carries. Nothing in the generator enumerates
them.

## What KSP generates

A dispatcher that is an exhaustive `when` over state then action, calling your
members for `HANDLE` cells and building the static ones inline. For the Timer,
the shape is exactly this (hand-written in the kotlinc-only example, so it
can be read without running Gradle). An interface with one member per
`HANDLE` cell and per effect, then a `when` with no `else`:

MD
code $K/examples/02-timer/src/Timer.kt kotlin '^interface Cells \{' '^\}'
code $K/examples/02-timer/src/Timer.kt kotlin '^fun step\(' '^\}'
prose <<'MD'
The `when` is in generated code only. Your file has no `when` to put an `else`
in, which is what turns Kotlin's weakest guarantee -- exhaustiveness that one
`else` defeats -- into one as strong as Rust's.

## When a cell is missing

MD
expect_of $K/compile_fail/missing_cell.kt
prose <<'MD'
kotlinc's own message, required by a fixture on every run. Declaration
mistakes -- an unknown state in a `@Row`, a row with the wrong number of cells
-- are refused by the processor with a `tabular-center::` code and a pointer at the
annotation ([Diagnostics](diagnostics)).

## Composition and suspend

`Kind.DELEGATE` names the child's annotated interface; the parent requires the
child's whole surface:

MD
code $K/examples/06-generated/src/Job.tb.kt kotlin '^@Machine\(' '^\}'
prose <<'MD'
Make the prototype `suspend fun handle(...)` and `step`, `perform` and every
member suspend; `SuspendDriver` runs it with no coroutine library, from
`tabular-center-core` alone. A suspending child under a plain parent does not compile.

## Setting it up

Not on Maven Central yet. The examples consume the repository the way you can
today: the runtime as sources, the processor as an included build.

MD
code $K/examples/06-generated/build.gradle.kts kotlin '^plugins \{' '^\}'
code $K/examples/06-generated/build.gradle.kts kotlin '^sourceSets\["main"\]\.kotlin\.srcDir\("src"\)' 'srcDir\("\.\./\.\./annotations"\)'
code $K/examples/06-generated/build.gradle.kts kotlin '^dependencies \{' '^\}'
code $K/examples/06-generated/settings.gradle.kts kotlin '^includeBuild\(' '^\}'
prose <<'MD'
The planned artifacts are `tabular-center-core` (runtime), `tabular-center-annotations`
(compile-only), `tabular-center-ksp` (the processor) and `tabular-center-testing` (a `.tbl`
fixture harness), so a production build carries the runtime and nothing else.

## Examples

